Synthetic private trade-socket frames and REST answers (decision 0009). Nothing in this
directory was received from QFEX or belongs to an account.

- Each JSON file but the REST answers below is one frame, hand-built with the member
  names QFEX's trade AsyncAPI (https://docs.qfex.com/websocket/trade.yaml) gives OrderResponse,
  AllOrdersResponse (its orders and twaps rows), FillResponse, PositionPayload, BalancePayload
  and StopOrder, in the envelopes its channel pages show (order_response is the order responses
  page's example, https://docs.qfex.com/websocket/channels/trade/order, with its values
  replaced).
- Every value is made up: the UUIDs are repeated digits, the user_id and id values name no user
  or account, and the symbols, prices, quantities, times and enum values (execution_type,
  direction, stop_order_type, a TWAP's status) are invented, not taken from a capture or
  checked against QFEX. They prove which bytes the redaction names, and, for
  all_orders_response.json, how the probe's page decoder reads the documented shape
  (crates/venues/fbc-venue-qfex/tests/account_reads.rs), not what QFEX sends.
- subaccounts.json, positions.json and positions_flat.json (FBC-h3z1) are hand-built REST
  answers in the shapes QFEX's REST reference gives ListSubaccountsResponseBody
  (https://docs.qfex.com/api-reference/rest/user/list-subaccounts) and
  PositionsBalanceResponseBody with its BalancePublic and PositionPublic
  (https://docs.qfex.com/api-reference/rest/user/user-positions), read 2026-11-05. Neither page
  shows an example answer. The account ids are repeated digits naming no account, the $schema
  URLs are on a reserved example domain, and every balance, position, margin and count is
  invented; the positions are chosen to pin exact decoding (1e-12, a twenty-digit position, and
  0, 0.0 and -0.0 as flat), not taken from a capture.
- user_trades_response.json (FBC-be57) is one frame in the envelope the trade AsyncAPI gives
  the get_user_trades reply, user_trades_response, an array of TradePayload. Its first row
  carries exactly TradePayload's members (trade_id, symbol, price, quantity, side, order_id,
  fee, order_type, tif); its second carries exactly the members of the example row on the
  get_user_trades page (https://docs.qfex.com/websocket/channels/trade/get_user_trades:
  trade_id, symbol, price, quantity, aggressor_side, order_id, client_order_id, fee,
  timestamp), both read 2026-11-16. The two rows sit in one page to pin how the probe's trades
  reader keeps each field's presence, not because QFEX sends both shapes at once.
- user_trades.json (FBC-be57) is one frame in the envelope the get_user_trades page gives its
  answer (the same page, read 2026-11-16): user_trades, an array of rows, beside count. Its one
  row carries exactly that page's example row's members. Its fee and timestamp carry more
  significant digits than an f64 holds, to pin that the reader keeps them exactly as written.
- historic_orders.json (FBC-be57) is a hand-built REST answer in the shape QFEX's REST
  reference gives HistoricOrdersResponseBody and HistoricOrder
  (https://docs.qfex.com/api-reference/rest/user/historic-orders, read 2026-11-16): a $schema,
  count and data, and each order's sixteen required members. The page shows no example answer
  and lists no terminal_status or execution_state value, so CANCELLED, FILLED and
  SYNTHETIC_STATE are invented.
- In all three, the UUIDs are repeated digits naming no order or trade, the client_order_id values
  name no order, SYNTH-USD is no QFEX symbol, the $schema URL is on a reserved example domain,
  and every price, quantity, fee and time is invented.

This marker covers fixtures/qfex/private/ only. A recorded frame never goes here.
